<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isthmus of Panama</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isthmus of Darién</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> is the narrow strip of land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> that links North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a> crossed it in 1513, and it became a key route for Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s Darien scheme tried to found a colony there in 1698–1700 and failed disastrously, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> was opened across it in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-panama.jpg" alt="Isthmus of Panama" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsthmusOfPanama.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, historically known as the Isthmus of Darien, is the narrow strip of land between the Caribbean Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> that links North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. The country of Panama and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> lie on it. It is generally thought to have formed around 3 million years ago, separating the Atlantic and Pacific and, in the view first put forward by Henry Fairfield Osborn in 1910, giving rise to the Gulf Stream. Its closing allowed animals and plants to cross between the continents in what is called the Great American Interchange.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a>, a Spanish conquistador, was the first European to reach the Pacific from the isthmus, on 25 September 1513. The town of Panamá was founded in 1519 on the Pacific coast, and in 1671 the Welsh privateer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morgan">Henry Morgan</a> crossed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> side and destroyed it. The ruins of Panamá Viejo were declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1997.</p><p>Spanish conquest was ruinous for the local Cueva people. A regional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, deadly working conditions, wars of conquest and the destruction of communities led to their dispersal, and by 1550 fewer than 300 indigenous people were counted in the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a>, Nombre de Dios and Nata combined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> and gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> crossed the isthmus overland to Porto Bello.</p><p>In 1698 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> tried to found a colony called New Caledonia on the Gulf of Darién; the scheme was unsuccessful. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> gold rush from 1849 sent travellers across the isthmus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-de-lesseps">Ferdinand de Lesseps</a> started a canal company in 1880 that went bankrupt in 1889. In 1902–1904 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> to grant independence to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Isthmus, bought the company's assets and finished the canal in 1914.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isthmus of Panama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…economic weakness grew acute: the famines of the 1690s, known as the ill years, and the failure of the Darien scheme, a colony on the <b>Isthmus of Panama</b> in 1698–1700 that lost a large share of the country’s capital, convinced many that access to English markets was essential.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Vasco Núñez de Balboa climbed a peak on the <b>Isthmus of Panama</b> and sighted the 'South Sea'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
